Section 3: Intervention & AI Methods
	•	Primary ML Component: Yes
	•	Study Design: Retrospective Cohort
	•	AI Model Architecture: Convolutional Neural Network (CNN)
	•	Algorithm Name: Self-supervised learning attention-based multiple instance learning pipeline using RetCCL encoder and Marugoto; comparator CRANE
	•	Input Variables (Data Modality): Pathology slides
	•	Comparator / Standard of Care: Human expert/Clinician
	•	Validation Method: External Validation (Geographic/Different Hospital)
	•	Explainability / Interpretability Used: Yes (e.g., SHAP, LIME)
	•	Feature Selection Method: None/All features
	•	Hyperparameter Tuning Reported: Yes
	•	Section 3 Comments/Quotes: The authors trained “our own deep learning pipeline using an SSL-based feature extractor combined with attention-based MIL” on H&E whole-slide pathology images and compared it against the CRANE method. The ground truth came from “two expert pathologists… grading the degree of rejection in consensus,” so human expert is the best comparator. Validation included hold-out and separate cohorts from other German centers, so external geographic is the strongest label. Interpretability came from attention maps and Grad-CAM visualizations. The paper reports optimizer choice, learning-rate finding, early stopping, and training settings, which count as hyperparameter/training-procedure reporting.  ￼

Section 4: AI Quality & Reproducibility (CONVINCE Standards)
	•	Missing Data Handling: Complete Case Analysis (Excluded)
	•	Class Imbalance Addressed: No
	•	Code Availability: Algorithm/Model weights available
	•	Data Preprocessing / Normalization Described: Yes
	•	Training Size (N): 393
	•	Test Size (N): 686
	•	Section 4 Comments/Quotes: Only samples “with information on ISHLT grading” were eligible, which is most consistent with complete-case inclusion. The primary training cohort had 393 slides, and the three validation cohorts had 356, 189, and 141 slides, totaling 686 test slides. The paper describes digitization, tessellation, patch extraction, feature extraction, bag construction, and training procedures. No class-imbalance correction was described. The code repositories for preprocessing and deep learning are explicitly provided, satisfying code availability. The retrieved text did not explicitly mention released trained weights, but the available option set requires one of the listed labels; code availability is clearly affirmative.  ￼

